  "poe": {
    "budgetW": 380,
    "sourceUrl": "https://apps.juniper.net/hct/product/EX2300%20Multigigabit/hwspecs",
    "note": "380 W from a 535 W fixed supply, quoted identically by the per-model hardware specs, the power-supply table and the datasheet. All 24 access ports are PSE ports at 30 W each, so 380 W covers 12 x 30 W PoE+ and the other 12 at af. 802.3af/at only, including on the multi-gigabit ports."
  },
  "fabric": {
    "capacityMbps": 128000,
    "kind": "packet-switching capacity",
    "note": "Datasheet figure: 128 Gbps, because the datasheet groups the 24MP with the 24-port models. Juniper's per-model hardware specs give this model 76 Gbps unidirectional / 152 Gbps bidirectional instead - a direct contradiction between two Juniper documents. The datasheet figure is recorded and the conflict is stated in the PR body; 95 Mpps is the throughput both documents share."
  },
  "sourceUrl": "https://apps.juniper.net/hct/product/EX2300%20Multigigabit/hwspecs",
  "notes": "Port numbering is not contiguous by rate: the eight multi-gigabit ports are 0-7 (mge-0/0/0-7 in the datasheet's Table 1) and the sixteen gigabit ports are 8-23, which is why they are listed explicitly instead of generated from one template. The multi-gigabit ports are modelled at 2.5G: the datasheet calls them 1/2.5 Gbps ports and Juniper's quick start describes this model as 16 x 10/100/1000BASE-T plus 8 x 10/100/1000/2500BASE-T. 10 in / 25.4 cm deep, 3.99 kg, three fixed side-to-side fans, 45.7 dB, 8 GB flash against 2 GB on the non-multigigabit models. This model first shipped in 18.1R2, and PoE was only enabled on it from that release."
